1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:02,049
Friday, November 13.

2
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:05,769
2.54 in the afternoon, central time.

3
00:00:06,330 --> 00:00:10,339
The United States did something it almost never does.

4
00:00:10,679 --> 00:00:13,769
It stopped every departing flight in the country.

5
00:00:14,230 --> 00:00:15,689
not because of a storm.

6
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:17,510
not because of an attack.

7
00:00:18,010 --> 00:00:22,850
because the people who guide planes through the sky had a problem talking to them.

8
00:00:23,350 --> 00:00:24,449
in this video.

9
00:00:24,699 --> 00:00:26,760
what we know about the outage.

10
00:00:27,059 --> 00:00:28,550
why it keeps happening.

11
00:00:28,859 --> 00:00:31,530
and the question nobody has answered yet.

12
00:00:32,179 --> 00:00:35,649
How much money does a single afternoon like this burn?

13
00:00:36,179 --> 00:00:37,670
Here's what's confirmed.

14
00:00:38,170 --> 00:00:42,979
The Federal Aviation Administration ordered a nationwide ground stop.

15
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:47,909
what it called a temporary telecommunications outage.

16
00:00:48,759 --> 00:00:53,729
Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy said it was "a communication issue."

17
00:00:54,009 --> 00:00:57,450
impacting all air route traffic control centers.

18
00:00:57,820 --> 00:01:02,570
Those are the big regional centers that handle planes at cruising altitude.

19
00:01:02,869 --> 00:01:04,579
across the whole country.

20
00:01:05,049 --> 00:01:08,329
To understand how big that is, look at a normal day.

21
00:01:08,819 --> 00:01:15,909
According to the FAA's own numbers, its air traffic system handles more than 44,000 flights.

22
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:20,609
and more than 3 million airline passengers every single day.

23
00:01:20,950 --> 00:01:24,900
across more than 29 million square miles of airspace.

24
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:27,049
every one of those flights.

25
00:01:27,609 --> 00:01:29,250
depends on a simple thing.

26
00:01:29,590 --> 00:01:32,969
A controller on the ground can talk to the pilot in the air.

27
00:01:33,459 --> 00:01:36,810
voice over radio and data over phone

28
00:01:37,079 --> 00:01:42,469
and network lines that link radar sites, towers, and control centers.

29
00:01:42,969 --> 00:01:44,359
when those links fail.

30
00:01:44,859 --> 00:01:48,840
Controllers can lose the ability to talk to flights or to see them.

31
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:55,329
And when controllers can't safely talk to planes, the FAA does the only safe thing it can.

32
00:01:55,859 --> 00:01:57,890
it stops sending more planes up.

33
00:01:58,390 --> 00:01:59,560
That's a ground stop.

34
00:01:59,829 --> 00:02:05,219
Nothing new takes off until the people in charge are sure every flight can be guided.

35
00:02:05,780 --> 00:02:08,740
Planes already in the air were allowed to keep flying.

36
00:02:09,050 --> 00:02:09,729
and land.

37
00:02:10,009 --> 00:02:12,039
new departures were frozen.

38
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:15,169
international flights heading to the US.

39
00:02:15,419 --> 00:02:17,479
were handled case by case.

40
00:02:17,819 --> 00:02:19,430
and by Friday evening.

41
00:02:19,770 --> 00:02:24,650
The FAA said the stop would last until at least 11pm Eastern.

42
00:02:25,110 --> 00:02:27,169
The first question everyone asked.

43
00:02:27,580 --> 00:02:28,840
Was this an attack?

44
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:31,270
So far officials say no.

45
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:36,129
The White House said there was no indication of a nefarious act.

46
00:02:36,629 --> 00:02:45,310
Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin said it was not a threat to the country and not linked to this week's Microsoft outage.

47
00:02:45,780 --> 00:02:52,199
Bloomberg reported that investigators were looking at whether a software update could be behind it.

48
00:02:52,699 --> 00:02:54,409
that has not been confirmed.

49
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:59,909
If the FAA announces an official cause, it will be pinned in the description.

50
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:06,979
By the afternoon, the flight tracking site FlightAware counted nearly 7,000 flights delayed.

51
00:03:07,259 --> 00:03:07,969
or canceled.

52
00:03:08,409 --> 00:03:10,629
at Chicago's two airports alone.

53
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,060
more than a thousand delays.

54
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:16,039
and the ground stop doesn't end when it's lifted.

55
00:03:16,599 --> 00:03:19,849
Planes and crews end up in the wrong cities.

56
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:23,169
pilots hit their legal limit on working hours.

57
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:27,240
The next morning's first flights may not have a plane or a crew.

58
00:03:27,770 --> 00:03:30,530
One afternoon can ripple into the whole weekend.

59
00:03:30,969 --> 00:03:31,430
now.

60
00:03:31,770 --> 00:03:34,090
The question this channel exists for.

61
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:36,969
How much does a day like this cost?

62
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:39,050
Here's the honest answer.

63
00:03:39,379 --> 00:03:42,500
Nobody has published a number for this outage yet.

64
00:03:43,259 --> 00:03:46,599
Anyone giving you an exact figure today is guessing.

65
00:03:47,219 --> 00:03:48,870
but we do have a yardstick.

66
00:03:49,340 --> 00:03:54,889
The FAA has paid for studies on what flight delays cost the US economy.

67
00:03:55,319 --> 00:03:56,650
for 2019.

68
00:03:57,139 --> 00:04:00,969
That estimate was about $33 billion a year.

69
00:04:01,590 --> 00:04:06,340
That counts what airlines lose, what passengers lose in wasted time.

70
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:07,750
Lost Bookings

71
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:10,469
and the knock-on effect on the wider economy.

72
00:04:10,939 --> 00:04:13,699
Divide that by 365.

73
00:04:13,939 --> 00:04:18,149
and it's roughly $90 million a day on an ordinary day.

74
00:04:18,709 --> 00:04:20,100
ordinary delays.

75
00:04:20,379 --> 00:04:22,339
Friday was not an ordinary day.

76
00:04:22,709 --> 00:04:27,649
So the real bill for this outage is almost certainly higher than a normal day.

77
00:04:28,019 --> 00:04:29,129
How much higher?

78
00:04:29,399 --> 00:04:31,779
we'll only know when the airlines report.

79
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:33,800
So who pays?

80
00:04:34,170 --> 00:04:44,569
In the original FAA funded study, about half of the cost of delays fell on passengers, not airlines, missed meetings, extra hotel nights,

81
00:04:44,730 --> 00:04:47,939
lost work hours, a wedding you didn't make it to,

82
00:04:48,220 --> 00:04:50,279
Airlines pay for crews.

83
00:04:50,740 --> 00:04:59,259
Fuel burned while waiting, rebooking and hotel vouchers. And then there's everyone else. Hotels, rental cars.

84
00:04:59,350 --> 00:05:02,569
and the businesses waiting for people who never arrived.

85
00:05:02,970 --> 00:05:05,189
One more thing passengers should know.

86
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:08,709
when the cause is an air traffic control problem.

87
00:05:09,050 --> 00:05:10,819
and not the airline itself,

88
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:15,620
US airlines generally don't have to cover your hotel or your meals.

89
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:17,350
Some do it anyway.

90
00:05:17,689 --> 00:05:20,259
Check your airline's policy before you assume.

91
00:05:20,790 --> 00:05:22,920
But there is one thing you are owed.

92
00:05:23,189 --> 00:05:25,250
no matter whose fault it was.

93
00:05:25,560 --> 00:05:27,170
since 2024.

94
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:31,720
U.S. rules require airlines to give you an automatic cash refund.

95
00:05:32,089 --> 00:05:33,889
if your flight is cancelled.

96
00:05:34,259 --> 00:05:36,230
or significantly changed.

97
00:05:36,629 --> 00:05:38,500
and you choose not to travel.

98
00:05:38,839 --> 00:05:40,740
significantly changed means.

99
00:05:41,139 --> 00:05:42,470
for a domestic flight.

100
00:05:42,839 --> 00:05:46,050
leaving or arriving more than three hours late.

101
00:05:46,519 --> 00:05:48,040
for an international flight.

102
00:05:48,410 --> 00:05:49,509
more than 6.

103
00:05:49,980 --> 00:05:52,230
and the money has to come back fast.

104
00:05:52,819 --> 00:05:56,389
within 7 business days if you paid by credit card.

105
00:05:56,949 --> 00:05:59,720
within 20 days for other payment methods.

106
00:06:00,089 --> 00:06:01,060
Not a voucher.

107
00:06:01,660 --> 00:06:02,850
not a travel credit.

108
00:06:03,189 --> 00:06:04,420
unless you accept one.

109
00:06:05,269 --> 00:06:05,730
cash.

110
00:06:06,389 --> 00:06:07,430
to the way you paid.

111
00:06:07,829 --> 00:06:11,879
So if your flight was cancelled on Friday and you decided to stay home.

112
00:06:12,379 --> 00:06:15,970
Don't let anyone talk you into a credit you didn't ask for.

113
00:06:16,439 --> 00:06:17,930
This isn't the first time.

114
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:20,870
It's the first nationwide ground stop.

115
00:06:21,110 --> 00:06:23,170
since January 2023.

116
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:27,850
That time, a system that sends safety notices to pilots,

117
00:06:28,120 --> 00:06:29,160
called NOTAM,

118
00:06:29,689 --> 00:06:30,310
crashed.

119
00:06:30,709 --> 00:06:34,019
The cause was traced to a damaged database file.

120
00:06:34,649 --> 00:06:37,060
More than a thousand flights were cancelled.

121
00:06:37,399 --> 00:06:39,459
and about 10,000 were delayed.

122
00:06:39,990 --> 00:06:42,439
in 2025, controllers.

123
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:44,329
handling Newark Airport.

124
00:06:44,629 --> 00:06:47,139
lost radar and radio contact.

125
00:06:47,449 --> 00:06:48,680
more than once.

126
00:06:49,079 --> 00:06:50,120
part of the problem.

127
00:06:50,490 --> 00:06:54,660
radar data traveling over old copper telecom wires

128
00:06:55,029 --> 00:06:56,680
in September of this year.

129
00:06:56,980 --> 00:07:03,300
The same Philadelphia facility lost both its main and its backup data lines at the same time.

130
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:07,689
Newark had almost no commercial landings for much of that day.

131
00:07:07,990 --> 00:07:11,649
and now every air route traffic control center in the country.

132
00:07:12,220 --> 00:07:13,990
The pattern is the same every time.

133
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:15,560
old equipment.

134
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:18,250
and backups that fail when they're needed.

135
00:07:18,779 --> 00:07:28,160
Back in 2023, the FAA's own budget documents described the NOTAM database as running on failing vintage hardware.

136
00:07:28,819 --> 00:07:31,370
That's the agency describing itself.

137
00:07:31,899 --> 00:07:36,839
Congress approved $12.5 billion last year to modernize

138
00:07:37,180 --> 00:07:38,569
air traffic control.

139
00:07:39,350 --> 00:07:42,889
The FAA says it has already replaced.

140
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:49,959
1346 communication lines and 190 voice switches

141
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:55,269
But Secretary Duffy has said finishing the job will take tens of billions more.

142
00:07:55,670 --> 00:07:59,939
so the money is moving, but the system is being repaired while it's running.

143
00:08:00,339 --> 00:08:03,009
like changing the engine of a plane in mid-flight.

144
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:05,319
Here's what we still don't know.

145
00:08:05,750 --> 00:08:07,620
We don't know the official cause.

146
00:08:07,959 --> 00:08:11,850
We don't know the final count of delays and cancellations.

147
00:08:12,089 --> 00:08:14,050
once the ripple effect is over.

148
00:08:14,459 --> 00:08:16,100
and we don't know the bill.

149
00:08:16,439 --> 00:08:21,639
No airline or government agency has put a dollar figure on this day yet.

150
00:08:22,009 --> 00:08:28,389
A country can spend billions building the safest air system in the world and still lose an afternoon.

151
00:08:28,660 --> 00:08:32,490
and millions of dollars to a phone line that stops working.

152
00:08:33,019 --> 00:08:35,110
This story is still developing.

153
00:08:35,509 --> 00:08:41,639
Everything here is based on official statements and news reports from November 13th.

154
00:08:42,039 --> 00:08:43,269
2026.

155
00:08:43,830 --> 00:08:46,120
when the airlines and the FAA

156
00:08:46,490 --> 00:08:47,970
put a number on this.

157
00:08:48,279 --> 00:08:49,610
I'll do the follow up.

158
00:08:50,259 --> 00:08:52,129
Subscribe if you want to see it.

159
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:56,769
and tell me in the comments were you stuck in an airport on Friday?
